{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: please review exact head a3bf832ef1fd8af73c00d4d9c3783628ab8c2f13 on request/merge-driven-succession, pushed and clean, against primary artifact 514ab482 at cmd/gs, with live candidates 8c28cb04 at internal/workroom and 0217b3c5 at internal/app. All three are effective, live and not stale and name that exact head; current main 0e0e71ad is merged in.\n\nThis closes the one blocking finding of your changes-requested review bd5f276e: the author of an approved implementation could invent a merge head and a retirement plan, publish a successor at any path, and retire another actor's artifact anywhere in the log. Your probe is now a test, TestMergeReceiptFromTheImplementerReachesNoUnrelatedTree, in the shape you ran it: an approval for spike, an invented merge head, an operator-authored victim at docs, a self-published docs successor. Removing the fix returns the exact verdict you reported, effective with reason \"merge approval authorized artifact succession\"; with it the supersession is ineffective, \"actor may not supersede target\", and the victim stays live.\n\nThe design, and its cost, are the parts worth your attention. The fold is pure over records and holds no repository, so it can open no merge head, read no diff, and establish that no merge happened at all — I considered giving it Git access and rejected it, because a fold that reads a repository is no longer a function of the log and no longer agrees between two clones. Every other field of a receipt is written by the actor asking for the authority. So the bound comes from the only party who did not write it: the reviewer. The artifact your approval names fixes the path lineage the receipt can reach — that path, inside it, or a directory containing it. The consequence is that a merge touching several areas carries cross-author authority only within the approved artifact's tree, and predecessors elsewhere stay with their authors or with an actor holding ratifier, where they were before merge succession existed. gs merge applies the same bound before HEAD moves, naming the target, its path and the approved tree, so such a plan is refused with the target unchanged rather than landing and stopping half-way. Please say whether you accept that narrowing as the honest contract, or want a different one.\n\nTwo filing notes. No docs or internal/docset candidate is published this round: every live artifact at each of those exact paths is either stale or an unmerged candidate on another line, so resting on one would make an unmergeable candidate and inventing a basis would make provenance false — the reasoning you accepted for internal/docset in bd5f276e, now true of docs as well after main moved. And this head is what a merger would land, so its own merge will need an approval naming an artifact that covers what it retires, or hugh to merge it; that is the narrowing working, not a defect, but it is worth you seeing before you approve.\n\nDo not merge. Everything the earlier reviews confirmed is still green and still mutation-proved: citation-preserving succession versus orphaning, pre-HEAD atomicity, the sealed retry plan, the wider-directory rule, unrelated-candidate protection, bystander rejection, successor coverage, the no-successor rule, and the state@1 admission and state@0 closure.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:514ab482d8b1f75d277d6b33e73ffdfc428dffdc","conditions":"The implementer-forgery finding of review bd5f276e is closed at this exact head: a receipt signed by the approved implementation's author reaches only the path lineage of the artifact the approval names, the exact probe from that review is a regression test that fails effective without the fix and passes ineffective with it, and gs merge refuses an unreachable cross-author retirement before HEAD moves. Every protection confirmed by the earlier reviews stays green, and the architecture and merge pages state only what the fold enforces, including that it can verify no merge head, diff or tree. Verdict on the exact head; do not merge.","head":"a3bf832ef1fd8af73c00d4d9c3783628ab8c2f13","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}